# O1 Check Performance & Complexity Rule

Before returning non-trivial TypeScript/JavaScript code, evaluate **production performance risk**
as if running an O(n) complexity review against the change.

## Evaluate before returning code

- Cyclomatic complexity, nesting depth, and function length
- Nested loops over large lists or DOM trees
- Unbounded re-renders from unstable object/array references in React
- Sequential awaits that could be batched with `Promise.all`
- O(n²) lookups where a `Map` / `Set` would be O(n)

## Avoid in production paths

- Filtering or sorting large arrays on every render without memoization
- Deeply nested loops with `.includes()` / `.find()` on arrays
- Fetching the same data repeatedly without caching or deduplication
- Inline anonymous functions/objects passed to memoized child components

## Prefer

- `useMemo` / `useCallback` for expensive derived state (only when profiling shows need)
- `Map` / `Set` pre-indexing before iteration
- `Promise.all` for independent async work
- Early returns to flatten nesting
- Pagination or virtualization for long lists

## When returning non-trivial code

Include a **short complexity note** (e.g. "O(n) single pass over items with Map lookup").

If estimated risk is **high** or **critical**, refactor before returning the code.
